==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|implien}}, {{m|enm|emplien}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|emplier}}, from {{der|en|la|implico|implicare|to infold, involve}}, from {{m|la|in||in}} + {{m|la|plico|plicare|to fold}}. {{doublet|en|employ|implicate}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪmˈplaɪ/|a=GenAm,RP}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-imply.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪ|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|im|ply}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|of a proposition}} to have as a [[necessary]] [[consequence]]; to lead to (something) as a consequence
#: {{coi|en|[[w:en:Correlation does not imply causation|Correlation does not '''imply''' causation]]}}
#: {{ux|en|The proposition that "all dogs are mammals" '''implies''' that my dog is a mammal.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2016|date=|author=Grigoriy Blekherman; Daniel Plaumann; Rainer Sinn; Cynthia Vinzant|title=Low-Rank Sum-of-Squares Representations on Varieties of Minimal Degree|journal=arXiv|url=http://arxiv.org/abs/1606.04387|doi=|volume=|issue=|pages=
|passage=Our upper bound is the best possible, and it '''implies''' the existence of low-rank factorizations of positive semidefinite bivariate matrix polynomials and representations of biforms as sums of few squares. }}
# {{lb|en|transitive|of a person}} to [[suggest]] by [[logical]] [[inference]]
#: {{ux|en|When I state that your dog is brown, I am not '''implying''' that all dogs are brown.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|of a person or proposition}} to [[hint]]; to [[insinuate]]; to [[suggest]] [[tacit]]ly and avoid a [[direct]] [[statement]]
#: {{ux|en|What do you mean "we need to be more careful with hygiene"? Are you '''implying''' that I don't [[wash]] my hands?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Gender and Women's Studies in Canada: Critical Terrain|author=Margaret Helen Hobbs; Carla Rice|year=2013|page=13|ISBN=0889614849|passage=The wrongminded notion of the feminist movement which '''implied''' it was anti-male carried with it the wrongminded assumption that all female space would necessarily be an environment where patriarchy and sexist thinking would be absent.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2019|title=The Diviners|author=Margaret Laurence |page= |ISBN=|passage=Naturally, the river wasn't wrinkled or creased at all— wrong words, '''implying''' something unfluid like skin, something unenduring, prey to age.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2022|author=[[w:R. F. Kuang|R. F. Kuang]]|title=[[w:Babel, or the Necessity of Violence|Babel]]|publisher=Harper''Voyager''|page=470|passage=Both French and English had once used ''parcel'' to refer to pieces of land that made up an estate, but when it evolved to '''imply''' an item of business in both, it retained its connotations of small fragmentariness in French, whereas in English it simply meant a package.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} to [[enfold]], [[entangle]].
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|1|4|passage=And in his bosome secretly there lay / An hatefull Snake, the which his taile vptyes / In many folds, and mortall sting '''implyes'''.}}

====Usage notes====
